When an ARC task has a distinctive colored seed in noisy grids, do not equate resemblance with the rule. Track the seed shape, eligible target values, and spatial generator as independent hypotheses. For every demo, enumerate the full input/output difference mask and preserve negative evidence: unchanged lookalikes, untouched target-valued cells, and the original seed.

Before another demo or a submission, replay the proposed generator against every pair, especially when seed shape, palette, or dimensions vary. A literal translated-template matcher that looks plausible on one example but fails the varied demos is falsified; do not patch it by shifting a few cells. Request additional examples only for explicit remaining alternatives and a specific discriminating observation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u055/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-mixed-demo-review/references/cellwise-mask-and-generator-validation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-demo-checkpoint", "file": "references/sparse-geometry-vs-template.md", "content": "# Sparse geometry versus motif copying

When a sparse grid contains a compact colored motif plus isolated same-color marks, the motif may cue a geometric operation rather than serve as a template to replicate. Record the exact motif anchor and every marker coordinate. Test whether aligned markers define endpoints/borders and whether intervening cells are completed; infer eligible orientation, endpoint class, and scope separately. Account for existing markers that remain unchanged. Compare this with literal template-occurrence predictions against every demo, including negative evidence, before acting. A varied second geometry can discriminate the candidate mechanisms; request it only when it could alter the prediction.
